A||||3||Richard Parnell||ATF Removal||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||01-18-2000||02:19 PM||||I'm going to put in a new slave and master for my clutch.  I used the ATF which worked for a while but I'm having the same problems but with a slower returning pedal.  My question is, how do I remove all the ATF before I replace with DOT 4 or 5?  Will just flushing with the DOT until all the ATF is out be enough or will the residual ATF still cause the deterioration of the seals?  Thanks ahead of time.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-18-2000||02:30 PM||||I used an air compressor. I took off the master and slave, stuck a rag under the master cylinder hose, and blew compressed air (50psi) into the slave end of the line. That got most of it, but there was a little residue that came out durring bleeding. Now the only thing coming out again is DOT3.<BR>If you don't have an air compressor I would assume bleeding out all the ATF would be ok. Seems to me like the ATF had to be in contact with the seals for several day to cause any damage.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||01-18-2000||07:21 PM||||I dont know if you can get all the atf out of everything without disassembling the master and slave cylinders. alcohol makes an economical flush solvent and it doesnt cause any damage to hoses or seals. BTW rubbing alcohol, not drinking alcohol.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-18-2000||07:35 PM||||He mentioned he was replacing both cylinders too, or else I would have recommended popping them open and cleaning them out.||||reg||14
